Constraints:
- Produce exactly one card per slot, in the same order as card_ids.
- Generate the full list in one response; do not split or stream per-card outputs.
- Keep policies mutually exclusive: each card should represent a distinct lever, target, or mechanism without overlapping other cards.
- Avoid near-duplicates by varying instruments, stakeholders, and implementation mechanisms across the set.
- Categories and tags must come from the allowed lists.
- Use 1-3 tags per card; addresses_tags should point to the main world problems or opportunities this policy would most naturally affect.
- Write in plain language for a smart lay audience. A policymaker should recognize the lever immediately, and a non-expert should understand what it does.
- Favor broader, tangible interventions over technical compliance machinery unless the slot clearly calls for a technical safeguard.
- Keep the focus on the lived economy: jobs, prices, business conditions, services, housing, trade, power, logistics, and public trust.
- Make each card legible at a glance:
  - title: 2-4 words, concrete and plain-language.
  - short_description: <= 60 characters, one crisp line.
  - description: 1-2 short sentences, <= 180 characters total.
- Do not write titles like "If X Happens..." or "Response To Y". These are policy options, not reactive instructions.
- Include a wide mix of scale and domain: local pilots, public procurement, national programs, tax changes, trade measures, labor supports, energy or housing moves, and institutional reforms.
- Ensure several cards are easy wins and several are expensive, high-impact bets.
- budget_cost is the gameplay purchase price and must be a real constraint:
  - Use integers 1-4 only.
  - A stage budget of 5 should force meaningful tradeoffs.
  - Include many 1-2 cost cards, a healthy number of 3 cost cards, and a smaller set of 4 cost cards.
- impact_rating is the expected upside if the policy is executed well:
  - 1 = narrow or local effect.
  - 3 = meaningful national or system effect.
  - 5 = rare, agenda-defining intervention.
- time_to_launch captures how quickly the policy can realistically get started; time_to_impact captures when people would feel the result.
- cost.notes should mention the main tradeoff in simple language: budget strain, slower permitting elsewhere, higher bills, political backlash, uneven take-up, or implementation drag.
- Keep prerequisites_policy_tags and synergy_policy_tags sparse. Most policies should stand alone.
- role_restrictions should usually be empty unless a scenario specifically calls for role-based play.
- Ensure at least a few cards mirror concrete archetypes in a broader, more readable form:
  - sovereign compute buildout
  - power and grid upgrades
  - training vouchers or wage insurance
  - audit or liability rules for high-risk AI
  - automation limits in a sensitive sector
  - household cash support
  - tax policy aimed at AI windfalls
  - public employment or transition programs
- art_prompt should be a concise instruction for a horizontal card illustration and should match the broad policy story, not internal mechanics.
- art_prompt must specify an edge-to-edge landscape illustration that fills the entire frame with no empty margins or floating card.

